<span class="ez-toc-title-toggle"><a href="#" class="ez-toc-pull-right ez-toc-btn ez-toc-btn-xs ez-toc-btn-default ez-toc-toggle" aria-label="Toggle Table of Content"><span class="ez-toc-js-icon-con"><span class=""><span class="eztoc-hide" style="display:none;">Toggle</span><span class="ez-toc-icon-toggle-span"><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="list-377408" width="20px" height="20px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M6 6H4v2h2V6zm14 0H8v2h12V6zM4 11h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2zM4 16h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" class="arrow-unsorted-368013" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="10px" height="10px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" version="1.2" baseProfile="tiny"><path d="M18.2 9.3l-6.2-6.3-6.2 6.3c-.2.2-.3.4-.3.7s.1.5.3.7c.2.2.4.3.7.3h11c.3 0 .5-.1.7-.3.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7zM5.8 14.7l6.2 6.3 6.2-6.3c.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7c-.2-.2-.4-.3-.7-.3h-11c-.3 0-.5.1-.7.3-.2.2-.3.5-.3.7s.1.5.3.7z"/></svg></span></span></span></a></span></div><nav><ul class='ez-toc-list ez-toc-list-level-1 ' ><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-1" href="#What_Are_Potato_Flakes" >What Are Potato Flakes?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-2" href="#How_Are_Potato_Flakes_Made" >How Are Potato Flakes Made?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-3" href="#Types_of_Potato_Flakes" >Types of Potato Flakes</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-4" href="#Uses_of_Potato_Flakes" >Uses of Potato Flakes</a><ul class='ez-toc-list-level-3' ><li class='ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-5" href="#In_Food_Processing" >In Food Processing</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-6" href="#In_Household_Cooking" >In Household Cooking</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-7" href="#In_Baking" >In Baking</a></li></ul></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-8" href="#Where_to_Buy_Potato_Flakes_Powder" >Where to Buy Potato Flakes Powder?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-9" href="#How_to_Choose_Good_Potato_Flakes" >How to Choose Good Potato Flakes?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-10" href="#Price_of_Potato_Flakes_Powder" >Price of Potato Flakes Powder</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-11" href="#Potato_Flakes_Manufacturers" >Potato Flakes Manufacturers</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-12" href="#Packaging_of_Potato_Flakes" >Packaging of Potato Flakes</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-13" href="#Are_Potato_Flakes_Starch_Flour_and_Mashed_Potato_Powder_the_Same" >Are Potato Flakes, Starch, Flour, and Mashed Potato Powder the Same?</a></li></ul></nav></div><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="What_Are_Potato_Flakes"></span><strong>What Are Potato Flakes?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p><a href="/product/dinweys-bulk-potato-flakes/">Potato flakes</a> are dehydrated potato products made by removing the water content from fresh potatoes while retaining their nutrients. This results in flake-shaped pieces, making them a key ingredient in the food processing and baking industries.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="How_Are_Potato_Flakes_Made"></span>How Are Potato Flakes Made?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Potato flakes are typically produced in large industrial facilities, as small factories cannot achieve the same level of quality. The production process involves several steps:</p><ol><li>Fresh potatoes are washed, peeled, and selected.</li><li>They are steamed and mashed.</li><li>The mashed potatoes are then dried into large flakes using a drum dryer.</li><li>These large flakes can be further processed into smaller sizes, usually between 3mm-6mm.</li></ol><p>It is known as potato flour when ground into a fine powder (100 mesh). Note that in some regions, potato flour refers to starch.</p><figure id="attachment_415-2" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-415-2" style="width: 398px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img src="/images/potato-flour-300x300.jpg" decoding="async" class="wp-image-415 " alt="potato flour" width="408" height="408" title="What is Potato Flakes Powder? 10 Things You Must not Know 3" /><figcaption id="caption-attachment-415-2" class="wp-caption-text">Potato Flour</figcaption></figure><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Types_of_Potato_Flakes"></span>Types of Potato Flakes<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>There are two main types:</p><ol><li><strong>Large flakes</strong>: Used in food processing for snacks and fried foods.</li><li><strong>Finely ground flakes (potato flour)</strong>: Commonly used in mashed potatoes and baking, such as in potato bread and pastries.</li></ol><p>Another related product is <strong>potato granules</strong>, made using a different drying method (spray drying), often used in similar applications.</p><hr /><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Uses_of_Potato_Flakes"></span><strong>Uses of Potato Flakes</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><h3><span class="ez-toc-section" id="In_Food_Processing"></span>In Food Processing<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p>Potato flakes are a key ingredient in many snack foods, especially in making composite chips. Their natural flavor and retained nutrients make them a popular addition in the production of puffed snacks and fried foods.</p><h3><span class="ez-toc-section" id="In_Household_Cooking"></span>In Household Cooking<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p>At home, potato flakes are primarily used to make mashed potatoes or simple baked goods. Due to their high water absorption and dispersibility, they can also be used as fish bait.</p><h3><span class="ez-toc-section" id="In_Baking"></span>In Baking<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p>In baking, potato flakes add a unique flavor and contribute to the Maillard reaction, enhancing the roasted potato taste. While they may not provide the structural stability of wheat flour, they significantly improve the taste and nutritional value of baked goods.</p><hr /><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Where_to_Buy_Potato_Flakes_Powder"></span><strong>Where to Buy Potato Flakes Powder?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Although edible, potato flakes are mainly a food processing ingredient and are not widely available in regular markets. You can purchase them online.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="How_to_Choose_Good_Potato_Flakes"></span>How to Choose Good Potato Flakes?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>High-quality potato flakes are free of discoloration or black spots. The color (whether white or yellow) doesn&#8217;t affect the quality. To assess the quality, you can perform a water absorption test: high-quality flakes will disperse quickly in warm water.</p><p>If you&#8217;re an industrial buyer, check the supplier&#8217;s major customers. For example, some companies supply high-quality potato flakes to brands like Pepsi and Orion.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Price_of_Potato_Flakes_Powder"></span>Price of Potato Flakes Powder<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>The price of bulk potato flakes varies between $1.60 &#8211; $2.50 USD per kilogram, depending on quality and origin. Prices fluctuate based on potato market conditions.</p><hr /><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Potato_Flakes_Manufacturers"></span><strong>Potato Flakes Manufacturers</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>The leading producers of potato flakes are located in Europe, North America, Russia, and China. Regional differences in flavor, diet, and potato varieties contribute to product variation.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Packaging_of_Potato_Flakes"></span>Packaging of Potato Flakes<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Industrial packaging typically comes in large bags (20kg, 25kg, or 800kg). Smaller packages are available for home use, although they may be labeled differently.</p><hr /><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Are_Potato_Flakes_Starch_Flour_and_Mashed_Potato_Powder_the_Same"></span><strong>Are Potato Flakes, Starch, Flour, and Mashed Potato Powder the Same?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><ul><li><strong>Potato flakes</strong> are dehydrated potatoes, retaining cellular structure and nutrients, with a moisture content of around 6%.</li><li><strong>Potato starch</strong> is extracted from broken potato cells and dried to 10% moisture. It&#8217;s used in cooking, food processing, baking, and industrial applications.</li><li><strong>Potato flour</strong> refers to finely ground potato flakes and is used in mashed potato powder production.</li><li><strong>Mashed potato powder</strong> is similar to potato flour but often contains added ingredients.</li></ul><p>We offer a variety of high-quality potato flakes and potato starch products. Our team of experts can provide valuable information and recommendations tailored to your specific production needs. As a supplier to both Pepsi and Orion, we are committed to consistent product quality and timely delivery.</p><p>Links about</p><p><a href="/about-us/"><strong><u><b>Find out more about us</b></u></strong></a></p><p><a href="/products/"><strong><u><b>Find Products</b></u></strong></a></p><p><a href="/contact-us/"><strong><u><b>Contact us</b></u></strong></a></p><blockquote class="wp-embedded-content" data-secret="PKF6RacCr6"><p><a href="/potato-flakes-and-granules/">Potato Flakes and Granules: Properties, Uses, and Differences</a></p></blockquote><p><iframe data-lazyloaded="1" src="about:blank" class="wp-embedded-content" sandbox="allow-scripts" security="restricted" style="position: absolute; visibility: hidden;" title="&#8220;Potato Flakes and Granules: Properties, Uses, and Differences&#8221; &#8212; Northern China Potato Production Expert" data-litespeed-src="/potato-flakes-and-granules/embed/#?secret=031UG7lxzw#?secret=PKF6RacCr6" data-secret="PKF6RacCr6" width="600" height="338" frameborder="0" marginwidth="0" marginheight="0" scrolling="no"></iframe></p><blockquote class="wp-embedded-content" data-secret="s4ZpRbevZL"><p><a href="/potato-deep-processing/">The Complete Guide to Potato Deep Processing</a></p></blockquote><p><iframe data-lazyloaded="1" src="about:blank" class="wp-embedded-content" sandbox="allow-scripts" security="restricted" style="position: absolute; visibility: hidden;" title="&#8220;The Complete Guide to Potato Deep Processing&#8221; &#8212; Northern China Potato Production Expert" data-litespeed-src="/potato-deep-processing/embed/#?secret=foZ2I0D9ow#?secret=s4ZpRbevZL" data-secret="s4ZpRbevZL" width="600" height="338" frameborder="0" marginwidth="0" marginheight="0" scrolling="no"></iframe></p>`} />
